window.SURE={"built":"2026-11-28","subjects":{"se":{"name":"Software Engineering","code":"CSC375","exam":"2026-12-10","totalQ":96,"totalM":645,"units":[{"n":10,"name":"Software Management","rank":1,"q":21,"m":145,"share":22,"cum":22,"hook":"Doubling the guests does not double the cooks, and doubling the cooks does not halve the time.","idea":"Management turns a wish into commitments: break the work down until each piece can be estimated, estimate by two independent methods, schedule the dependencies, keep a ranked risk list, and compare actual progress against the plan often enough to act. Effort grows faster than size because communication, coordination and rework grow with the number of people, not with the code; COCOMO expresses this through an exponent above one, so doubling size more than doubles effort, and duration rises only as the effort raised to a fraction, so a late large project cannot be rescued by schedule alone.","one":"Effort grows faster than size, so a plan is only honest while it is still being measured against reality.","gate":{"q":"Why is COCOMO effort not proportional to the number of lines of code?","a":"Effort grows faster than size because communication, coordination and rework grow with the number of people, not with the code. COCOMO expresses this through an exponent above one: about 1.05 in organic mode, 1.12 semi-detached and 1.20 embedded, so doubling size more than doubles effort. Duration rises only as the effort raised to a fraction, so a late large project cannot be rescued by schedule alone."},"keys":["Project management","Work breakdown structure","COCOMO","COCOMO modes","COCOMO equations","Function points"],"ask":[{"id":"se-15","q":"What is project management? Explain different project management activities in detail. Discuss risk management process with suitable example showing how risks are identified, analyzed, and mitigated in a software project.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":10},{"id":"se-2","q":"List some advantages of estimation. Explain COCOMO model.","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"se-25","q":"Explain the importance of software pricing. Highlight COCOMO cost modeling technique. List its disadvantage.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"se-37","q":"In the software development process, how does software configuration management facilitate the changes that may occur during different stages of a software development life cycle? Justify your explanation with example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r11","q":"In the software development process, how does software configuration management facilitate the changes that may occur during different stages of a software development life cycle? Justify your explanation with example.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r23","q":"Explain with example how COCOMO can be used for software cost estimation?","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":8,"name":"Software Testing","rank":2,"q":12,"m":90,"share":14,"cum":36,"hook":"A bus can pass every workshop check and still be too tall for the bridge on its route.","idea":"Testing compares what the system does against what it was supposed to do, with the expectation taken from the specification rather than from the code's own logic. Coverage counts the structure the tests walked through, not the requirements the system had to meet: a suite written from existing code cannot cover an omitted requirement, a wrong assumption about the user, or a timing and load fault that appears only when the whole system runs. Coverage is therefore a measure of confidence, not proof, and inspection plus system and acceptance testing remain necessary.","one":"Testing cannot prove a system has no bugs; it can only show which risks have actually been looked at.","gate":{"q":"Why can full branch coverage still leave a system that fails in use?","a":"Coverage counts the structure the tests walked through, not the requirements the system had to meet. A test suite written from existing code cannot cover a requirement that was omitted, a wrong assumption about the user, or a timing and load fault that appears only when the whole system runs. Coverage is therefore a measure of confidence, not proof, and inspection plus system and acceptance testing remain necessary."},"keys":["Verification","Validation","Software inspection","Unit testing","Integration strategies","System testing"],"ask":[{"id":"se-1","q":"List different stakeholders involved in software inspection. How software inspection is carried out? Explain","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"se-13","q":"What is software testing? Explain the software testing process in detail. Differentiate between development testing and release testing. How does Test-Driven Development (TDD) improve software quality? Explain with an example.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":10},{"id":"se-70","q":"What is clean room software development? Discuss the characteristics of cleanroom software development.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"se-71","q":"Discuss path testing with suitable example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r22","q":"Differentiate between verification and validation. Explain the software Inspection process.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":2,"name":"Software Processes","rank":3,"q":11,"m":85,"share":13,"cum":50,"hook":"A wedding kitchen that cooks the entire menu before tasting anything has bet the whole feast on one guess.","idea":"A process model fixes the order of the same four activities — specification, design and implementation, validation, evolution — plus who does them and what evidence closes each stage. Which model you follow is decided by the stability of the requirements and the shape of the contract, not fashion: waterfall signs off each phase and delivers once, so it gives milestones and a fixed price but pushes all integration risk into late testing; incremental delivery ships prioritised, usable slices, absorbing change early at the cost of shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration. Choosing against requirement volatility moves every correction to its most expensive point.","one":"A process model is a bet about how wrong your requirements will be; pick it by how much change you expect.","gate":{"q":"What decides whether a project should follow waterfall or be delivered incrementally?","a":"The stability of the requirements and the shape of the contract decide, not fashion. Waterfall signs off each phase and delivers once, so it gives milestones and a fixed price but pushes all integration risk into late testing. Incremental delivery ships prioritised, usable slices, absorbing change early at the cost of shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration. Choosing against requirement volatility moves every correction to its most expensive point."},"keys":["Software process","Four process activities","Waterfall","Incremental development","Integration and configuration","Throw-away prototyping"],"ask":[{"id":"se-27","q":"What do you understand by software process model? Explain different software process activities.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"se-3","q":"Why do you think software needs to be changed? Explain any one model that can be used to cope with the change.","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"se-50","q":"Explain the different software life cycle models and compare them with advantages and disadvantages.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":10},{"id":"se-62","q":"What is software process model? Discuss waterfall model with its merits and demerits.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r9","q":"Explain how prototyping model help in developing software? Differentiate between Evolutionary and throw-away prototype model.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":4,"name":"Requirements Engineering","rank":4,"q":11,"m":80,"share":12,"cum":62,"hook":"The customer says 'a loan for my shop'; the bank's file must say how much, at what rate, repaid by when.","idea":"Requirements engineering turns wishes into numbered, testable statements by asking the right people, negotiating conflicts, writing the result precisely and tracking every later change. Functional requirements say what the system does, and a missing one shows up as a feature that simply fails; non-functional requirements say how well it does it, so they decide the architecture and cannot be retrofitted by better coding later. They are also the ones written vaguely, which makes them untestable, so each must be given a measurable metric.","one":"A requirement is finished only when someone can write a test that would prove it was not met.","gate":{"q":"Why are non-functional requirements usually more critical than functional ones?","a":"Functional requirements say what the system does, and a missing one shows up as a feature that simply fails. Non-functional requirements say how well it must do it, so they decide the architecture and cannot be retrofitted by better coding later. They are also the ones written vaguely, which makes them untestable, so each must be given a measurable metric."},"keys":["Requirement","User requirement","System requirement","Functional requirement","Non-functional requirement","Domain requirement"],"ask":[{"id":"se-38","q":"Explain the main activities used in requirements engineering. What are the desirable characteristics of a good SRS documents? Explain with example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10},{"id":"se-51","q":"Differentiate between functional and non-functional requirements. What are the various types of functional and non-functional requirements that are placed on the system? Explain with an example.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":10},{"id":"se-64","q":"Briefly explain functional, non-functional, and domain requirements.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"se-66","q":"What is formal specification? Discuss interface specification in detail.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"System Modeling","rank":5,"q":12,"m":75,"share":12,"cum":74,"hook":"No single drawing explains a hospital: the referral letter, the OPD queue and the lab form each answer a different question.","idea":"A model is a deliberate simplification: keep what answers one question, drop the rest, and draw it in a notation everyone reads the same way. Context fixes the boundary, interaction fixes who talks to whom and in what order, structural models fix what exists, behavioural models fix how it changes over time. Because every view hides what the next needs, the models must be kept consistent with each other, and that comparison is where contradictions surface.","one":"A model is not a smaller system; it is the part of the system that answers one question.","gate":{"q":"Why does a complete system specification need several models instead of one diagram?","a":"Each model deliberately drops detail so that one question can be answered clearly. The context model fixes the boundary, use cases give actor goals, sequence diagrams fix message order, class diagrams fix structure, and state machines fix allowed behaviour. Because every view hides what the next needs, the models must be kept consistent with each other, and that comparison is where contradictions surface."},"keys":["System model","Context model","Use case diagram","Sequence diagram","Class diagram","State machine diagram"],"ask":[{"id":"se-26","q":"Draw use case diagram, class diagram and sequence diagram for online book recommendation system. Make your own assumptions.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"se-61","q":"Explain system modeling with suitable example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"se-69","q":"Discuss the importance of use case diagram in object-oriented development. Draw a use case diagram for library system.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"se-19","q":"Draw a context model and use case diagram for an Online Food Delivery System. Make necessary assumptions.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r21","q":"Differentiate between structural and behavioral models. What is the importance of behavioral model.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":6,"name":"Architectural Design","rank":6,"q":5,"m":45,"share":7,"cum":81,"hook":"You can repaint a hotel room in a week; you cannot move the lift shaft after the slab is poured.","idea":"Architectural design fixes the components, their responsibilities and the connections they are allowed before anyone writes detail code. The non-functional requirement the system cannot afford to lose decides, because each pattern buys one quality at the price of another: layering buys substitutability and adds latency; a repository buys shared consistent data and creates a bottleneck; client-server centralises control and creates one point of failure. The choice is defensible only when the accepted cost is written down.","one":"Architecture is the set of decisions that are expensive to reverse, and patterns are the ones others already paid for.","gate":{"q":"What decides which architectural pattern a system should be built on?","a":"The non-functional requirement the system cannot afford to lose decides, because each pattern buys one quality at the price of another. Layering buys substitutability and adds latency; a repository buys shared consistent data and creates a bottleneck; client-server centralises control and creates one point of failure. The choice is defensible only when the accepted cost is written down."},"keys":["Architectural design","4+1 views","Layered pattern","Client-server","MVC","Pipe and filter"],"ask":[{"id":"se-14","q":"Explain architectural design decisions that must be made during the architectural design process. Describe the following architectural patterns with suitable diagrams:","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":10},{"id":"se-39","q":"Explain architectural views. Illustrate on layered architecture, repository architecture, and pipe and filter architecture.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10},{"id":"se-67","q":"What are the activities of architectural design process? Discuss abstract machine model.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"se-68","q":"What is modular decomposition? Discuss object oriented model of decomposition.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Design and Implementation","rank":7,"q":8,"m":40,"share":6,"cum":87,"hook":"A cycle mended with welded parts runs today and cannot be repaired next year.","idea":"Design sets the seams: what a part hides inside and what it promises outside. Cohesion measures whether the responsibilities inside a component belong together, so high cohesion gives one reason to change; coupling measures how much one component depends on another, so low coupling stops a change from rippling outward. Information hiding reinforces both by exposing only a stable interface, and the consequence is that a part can be replaced without opening the system. Implementation keeps those seams honest with version control, review and refactoring.","one":"Good design is knowing where the cuts go: parts you can lift out without rebuilding the machine.","gate":{"q":"Why should cohesion be high and coupling low in a design that must survive change?","a":"Cohesion measures whether the responsibilities inside a component belong together, so high cohesion gives one reason to change; coupling measures how much one component depends on another, so low coupling stops a change from rippling outward. Information hiding reinforces both by exposing only a stable interface. The consequence is that a part can be replaced without opening the system."},"keys":["Cohesion","Coupling","Information hiding","Singleton","Adapter","Observer"],"ask":[{"id":"se-22","q":"What do you understand by implementation issues in software development? Discuss reuse, configuration management, and host-target development as implementation issues.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5},{"id":"se-24","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5},{"id":"se-31","q":"What do you understand by design patterns? What role does it have in object oriented design.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"se-52","q":"Explain the component base software engineering and its advantages.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Software Evolution","rank":8,"q":6,"m":30,"share":5,"cum":91,"hook":"The jeep that has carried goods to the village for twenty years is held together by patches, and cannot be retired.","idea":"Evolution treats change as the normal case: a request is logged, analysed for what else it touches, planned into a release, implemented and tested. Maintenance is classified by purpose — corrective, adaptive, perfective, preventive — because each needs a different share of the budget. Most post-delivery change is not repair but improvement: once a system works, users ask for more functions, better performance and new reporting, so perfective maintenance is about half of maintenance effort, against roughly twenty per cent corrective and twenty-five adaptive. Since maintenance is about sixty per cent of lifetime cost, structure and documented design decide whether that demand can be met affordably.","one":"A system nobody planned to change is a system that will soon be too expensive to change.","gate":{"q":"Why does perfective maintenance take the largest share of maintenance effort?","a":"Because most post-delivery change is not repair but improvement: once a system works, users ask for more functions, better performance and new reporting. Perfective maintenance is therefore about half of maintenance effort, against roughly twenty per cent corrective and twenty-five adaptive, with preventive the smallest. Since maintenance is about sixty per cent of lifetime cost, structure and documented design decide whether that demand can be met affordably."},"keys":["Software evolution","Corrective maintenance","Adaptive maintenance","Perfective maintenance","Preventive maintenance","Legacy system"],"ask":[{"id":"se-23","q":"Differentiate between corrective, adaptive, perfective, and preventive maintenance. Which type of maintenance consumes the most resources and why?","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5},{"id":"se-29","q":"“Software maintenance is one of the most importance activity.” Justify the statement with an example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"se-45","q":"What do you mean by legacy system? Explain its importance.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":5},{"id":"se-58","q":"Explain maintenance process in detail.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Agile Software Development","rank":9,"q":5,"m":30,"share":5,"cum":96,"hook":"A shop that fills its storeroom once a year is guessing; the one that restocks every week is reading its customers.","idea":"Agile keeps the work in short fixed windows that each end in something usable, and keeps the backlog ordered and open to change between windows. It fixes time and lets scope emerge, while a fixed-price contract fixes scope and price, so the two mechanisms contradict each other. Sprints re-rank the backlog and measure progress by working software, which leaves nothing stable to sign off and no up-front safety case; regulation needs requirements fixed, traceable and verified, so such projects keep plan-driven elements, and agile fails to scale without architecture and coordination.","one":"Agile replaces one forecast made in advance with a forecast rewritten every two weeks from evidence.","gate":{"q":"Why does agile delivery break down on a fixed-price contract for a safety-critical system?","a":"Agile fixes time and lets scope emerge, while the contract fixes scope and price, so the two mechanisms contradict each other. Sprints re-rank the backlog and measure progress by working software, which leaves nothing stable to sign off and no up-front safety case. Regulation needs requirements fixed, traceable and verified, so such projects keep plan-driven elements and agile fails to scale without architecture and coordination."},"keys":["Agile Manifesto","Sprint","Product backlog","Scrum roles","Velocity","XP practices"],"ask":[{"id":"se-86","q":"Explain the agile software development. Compare between agile software development with prototyping software development.","y":"SE Question Bank 2077","marks":10},{"id":"se-35","q":"What are the principles of agile development? Explain agile development techniques.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"se-42","q":"Differentiate plan driven and agile development.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":5},{"id":"se-5","q":"Differentiate between plan driven and agile development. Explain agile project management.","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r20","q":"Differentiate between plan driven and agile development. Explain agile project management.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","rank":10,"q":5,"m":25,"share":4,"cum":100,"hook":"One mason builds a house from memory; forty flats need drawings that outlive him.","idea":"Computer science asks what can be computed and how efficiently; software engineering asks how to deliver it to a cost, schedule and quality target with a team that will not stay forever. At scale, heterogeneity, legacy systems and constant business change defeat amateur programming, so process, documentation and explicit trade-offs appear — the written decisions that keep a large system buildable and changeable by people who never met its author. Because most lifetime cost is post-delivery, the discipline designs for change.","one":"Software engineering is the paperwork that keeps a big system changeable after the people who built it have left.","gate":{"q":"Why does software engineering exist as a discipline separate from computer science?","a":"Computer science asks what can be computed and how efficiently; software engineering asks how to deliver it to a cost, schedule and quality target with a team that will not stay forever. At scale, heterogeneity, legacy systems and constant business change defeat amateur programming, so process, documentation and explicit trade-offs appear. Because most lifetime cost is post-delivery, the discipline designs for change."},"keys":["Software","Four core quality attributes","Software engineering","SE vs computer science","SE vs system engineering","Maintenance share"],"ask":[{"id":"se-4","q":"Describe software engineering ethics thal needs to be followed.Why is it important ?","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":5},{"id":"se-40","q":"Explain software engineering ethics with example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":5},{"id":"se-57","q":"Differentiate between software engineering and computer science.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":5},{"id":"se-77","q":"Differentiate between software engineering and system engineering.","y":"SE Question Bank 2079","marks":5}],"reps":[]}]},"cdc":{"name":"Compiler Design and Construction","code":"CSC376","exam":"2026-12-13","totalQ":92,"totalM":695,"units":[{"n":2,"name":"Lexical Analysis and Syntax Analysis","rank":1,"q":35,"m":275,"share":40,"cum":40,"hook":"At the airport one officer only stamps slips, and the officer behind her decides whether the whole trip is legal.","idea":"Token shapes are regular, so a finite automaton recognises them with bounded memory. Sentence structure is context-free, so the parser needs a stack to match nested constructs. Splitting the two lets the scanner be generated from regular expressions and the parser from a grammar, and keeps each error message at the right level.","one":"The scanner classifies with bounded memory; the parser proves the sequence legal with a stack, because grammar nests.","gate":{"q":"Why can a finite automaton tokenise a program but not decide whether its brackets balance?","a":"A finite automaton has only a fixed number of states, so it remembers a bounded amount of context and cannot count unbounded nesting. Tokens are regular: each is matched by a pattern built from concatenation, union and star, which Thompson's construction turns into an NFA and subset construction into a DFA. Brackets and ifs nest to any depth, so matching them needs a stack, which the parser keeps."},"keys":["Token, lexeme, pattern","Thompson's construction","Subset construction","DFA minimisation","FIRST(α)","FOLLOW(A)"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-1","q":"Define important state. Why do we need augmented regular expression? Convert the regular expression “(a + b)*a(a + b)” to DFA.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-13","q":"What is the difference between LR(0), SLR(1), and LR(1) parsing? Explain with examples when each would fail or succeed.","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-14","q":"Explain the role of lexical analyzer in compiler design. What is input buffering? Describe the buffer-pair scheme with sentinels and explain why it is efficient.","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-25","q":"What is ambiguous grammar? Explain the “Dangling Else” problem with example. Show how to resolve it.","y":"CDC Model Set I","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r0","q":"Give an example of reduce-reduce conflict. Construct the SLR parsing table for the following grammar.","count":4,"marks":10},{"id":"r6","q":"Differentiate between recursive descent and non-recursive predictive parsing method. Find first and follow of all the non-terminals in the following grammar.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r17","q":"Compute the FIRST and FOLLOW of the non-terminals in the following grammar:S → (L) ∣ 1L → LS ∣∗S","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":7,"name":"Intermediate Code Generation","rank":2,"q":10,"m":75,"share":11,"cum":50,"hook":"The order says steam the momos; no single cook can execute a sentence like that, so it becomes numbered steps.","idea":"Lower every expression into instructions with at most one operator, each writing a named temporary. Nothing in that form mentions a processor, so one optimiser and one set of analyses serve every target, and identical subtrees can share a single computed value. Quadruples name the result explicitly, triples use the instruction number, and indirect triples add a pointer list so that steps can be reordered.","one":"Flatten the expression into one-operator steps with named bowls, so any back end can cook the same recipe.","gate":{"q":"Why lower the annotated tree to three-address code instead of generating machine instructions directly from it?","a":"A tree keeps grammar structure that no optimiser or target needs, and mapping it straight to one machine welds the language to that processor. Three-address code allows at most one operator per instruction with named temporaries, so a repeated subexpression can be computed once, moved or deleted, and the same form feeds every back end. Quadruples, triples and indirect triples are merely encodings of that flat list."},"keys":["Intermediate representation","Three-address code","Quadruple","Triple","Indirect triple","DAG"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-26","q":"What is backpatching? Why is it used in code generation? Generate three-address code with backpatching for the following code:","y":"CDC Model Set I","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-38","q":"What are the significances of intermediate code? Differentiate between DAG and Syntax tree. Represent the instruction A = B + C − D * E + G using quadruple and triple.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-55","q":"Define three address codes. write three address codes for","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-77","q":"Explain with example about different methods of intermediate code representation.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Syntax-Directed Translation","rank":3,"q":9,"m":70,"share":10,"cum":60,"hook":"The drawing proves the house is legal, but nothing in it yet says how much brick to order.","idea":"A syntax-directed definition attaches a semantic rule to every production. Attributes are synthesized when their value comes from children and inherited when it comes from the parent or an earlier sibling. If every attribute is synthesized, one postorder walk suffices; if inherited values depend only on the left, one left-to-right pass suffices, and that restricted form is what top-down translators can run.","one":"An SDD writes the meaning on the parse tree; the direction the numbers travel decides the evaluation order.","gate":{"q":"What decides whether a syntax-directed definition can be evaluated in a single pass over the parse tree?","a":"The direction attributes travel decides the order. A synthesized attribute is computed from the children and rises, so an S-attributed definition needs one post-order traversal, children before parents. An inherited attribute comes from the parent or a left sibling, and when it never depends on a right sibling the definition is L-attributed, so one left-to-right depth-first pass suffices for a predictive parser."},"keys":["Syntax-directed definition","Synthesized attribute","Inherited attribute","S-attributed definition","L-attributed definition","Annotated parse tree"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-15","q":"What is L-attributed definition? Differentiate between S-attributed and L-attributed definitions with examples.","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-3","q":"List some limitations of Recursive Descent Parsing. Construct a syntax directed translation scheme that translates an arithmetic expression from infix notation into postfix notation. Also create an annotated parse tree for the input 9 – 5 + 2.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-53","q":"Define Syntax directed definition. Construct annotated parse tree for the input expression (5*3+2)*5 according to the following","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-75","q":"Define Syntax directed definition. Construct annotated parse tree for the input expression (5*3+2)*5 according to the following","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r4","q":"Define inherited and synthesized attribute. Compute the FOLLOW set for the following grammar:","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r5","q":"Define Syntax directed definition. Construct annotated parse tree for the input expression (5*3+2)*5 according to the following","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to Compiler","rank":4,"q":8,"m":60,"share":9,"cum":69,"hook":"A letter written in Nepali must reach a reader who knows only English, and no single clerk can do the whole job.","idea":"A compiler is a pipeline of small specialists, each taking one representation and producing a simpler or more machine-like one. No single translator can be built, tested and retargeted, but one phase's output is the documented input of the next, so each can be proved and marked alone. Splitting at a machine-independent form lets n languages and m machines cost n plus m pieces instead of n times m translators, and each error is reported by the phase that can name it.","one":"A compiler is a chain of single-job clerks joined by a neutral draft that belongs to no language and no machine.","gate":{"q":"Why is a compiler built from several phases rather than one translator that maps source straight to machine code?","a":"Because no single translator can be built, tested and retargeted: one phase's output is the documented input of the next, so each can be proved and marked alone. Splitting at a machine-independent form lets n languages and m machines cost n plus m pieces instead of n times m translators, and each error is reported by the phase that can name it."},"keys":["Compiler","Interpreter","Analysis (front end)","Synthesis (back end)","Token","Symbol table"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-49","q":"Difference between compiler and interpreter. “Symbol table is necessary component of compiler”. Justify this statement with examples.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-71","q":"Explain briefly about different phases involved in compiler, with a block diagram","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-80","q":"Discuss the importance of error handler in compiler. How is it manipulated in the different phases of compilation?","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-84","q":"Difference between compiler and interpreter.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":8,"name":"Code Generation","rank":5,"q":8,"m":60,"share":9,"cum":78,"hook":"The recipe says mix, but the kitchen must still decide which pan is already hot on the stove.","idea":"Code generation chooses, for each intermediate instruction, real instructions and a home for every value. Basic blocks and their DAG show what is reused and what is dead inside a straight run; register allocation is an assignment of a small set of registers to many live values, solved by graph colouring or by heuristics with next-use information, spilling to memory when the pressure is too high. The first instruction, every jump target and every instruction immediately after a jump is a leader, so a jump may enter only at a leader and no branch lands in the middle of the block.","one":"Code generation is choosing real utensils: which values stay hot in registers, which get shelved, and which work is repeated.","gate":{"q":"How is a program split into basic blocks, and why must no branch enter a block except at its first instruction?","a":"The first instruction is a leader, every jump target is a leader, and every instruction immediately after a jump is a leader, so a block runs from a leader to the instruction before the next. A jump may enter only at a leader, so no branch lands in the middle, which keeps the block straight-line and lets its DAG expose repeated and dead computations before registers are assigned."},"keys":["Basic block","Leader","Flow graph","DAG of a block","Register allocation","Spilling"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-2","q":"How do you create a control flow graph from a basic block? Explain the need and criteria for code optimization.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-58","q":"Discuss about the different factors affecting target code generation.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-79","q":"Discuss about the different factors affecting target code generation.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-92","q":"Factors affecting (target code generator) code generator/code generator design issues","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r8","q":"Discuss about the different factors affecting target code generation.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":4,"name":"Type Checking","rank":6,"q":7,"m":50,"share":7,"cum":85,"hook":"A shop ledger that happily adds metres of pipe to kilograms of nails produces totals that look fine and a site that fails.","idea":"Types are the units of a program. A grammar constrains shape, not meaning, so adding a string to an integer derives from the addition and assignment productions while the parser accepts it. A type system gives every expression a type expression and demands that the operands of an operator agree, inserting only safe widening conversions and rejecting the rest unless the programmer writes an explicit cast. The mismatch is caught before code exists, so the error names its line instead of appearing as garbage at run time.","one":"Types are units: check the tag before every operation, convert only upward for free, and make the lossy direction explicit.","gate":{"q":"Why can a grammar accept a program that a type checker must reject?","a":"A grammar constrains shape, not meaning, so adding a string to an integer derives from the addition and assignment productions while the parser accepts it. A type system gives every expression a type expression and demands that the operands of an operator agree, inserting only safe widening conversions. The mismatch is caught before code exists, so the error names its line instead of appearing as garbage at run time."},"keys":["Type system","Static checking","Type expression","Type inference","Widening","Narrowing"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-54","q":"Differentiate between static and dynamic type checking. How can we carry out type checking for the following expression using syntax-directed defination?","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-76","q":"Write Syntax Directed Definition to carry out type checking for the following expression.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-83","q":"Type checking is the process of verifying that the types of expressions and variables used in a program are consistent and adhere to languages type system rules. The primary goal of type checking is to identify and prevent type-related errors before the program is executed.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-12","q":"Why do we need to check the type? Distinguish between DAG and syntax tree.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2082","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r7","q":"Differentiate between static and dynamic type checking. How can we carry out type checking for the following expression using syntax-directed defination?","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":9,"name":"Code Optimization","rank":7,"q":7,"m":50,"share":7,"cum":92,"hook":"A porter carries the same sack of rice up the hill twice because nobody told him it was already at the lodge.","idea":"Optimisation removes work the source does not require, but only when the visible result stays identical: it must preserve observable behaviour, so the result and every visible side effect stay the same. It must be profitable, since the analysis and rewriting consume compile time and code space and must repay that cost, and it must not change when errors are reported or hide a fault the source would have raised. Local rewrites need little proof, while global rewrites need data-flow facts to show that a value is still the same one; only then is a rewrite such as constant folding or hoisting a loop-invariant expression applied.","one":"Optimisation is not doing unnecessary work, and it is legal only when the observable result is bit for bit the same.","gate":{"q":"What three conditions must an optimisation satisfy, and why is each one necessary?","a":"It must preserve observable behaviour, so the result and every visible side effect stay identical. It must be profitable, since the analysis and rewriting consume compile time and code space and must repay that cost. It must not change when errors are reported or hide a fault the source would have raised. Only then is a rewrite such as constant folding or hoisting a loop-invariant expression applied."},"keys":["Code optimisation","Criteria","Constant folding","Constant propagation","Common subexpression elimination","Dead code elimination"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-56","q":"Define code optimization. Discuss about any three code optimization techniques with example.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-78","q":"What is the purpose of code optimization? Explain different types of loop optimization techniques with example.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-91","q":"What are the advantages of code optimization. Define Dead-code elimination.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-22","q":"What is peephole optimization? Explain the following peephole optimization techniques with examples:","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r16","q":"What is the purpose of code optimization? Explain different types of loop optimization techniques with example.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r27","q":"What is peephole optimization? Explain the following peephole optimization techniques with examples:","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":6,"name":"Run-time Storage Management","rank":8,"q":4,"m":30,"share":4,"cum":96,"hook":"The auditorium rents blocks of seats, and the last troupe to arrive is always the first to leave.","idea":"Every active call owns an activation record pushed on a stack, because calls nest and return in reverse order. The record holds the return address, saved registers, parameters, locals, temporaries and two links: the control link to the caller and the access link to the lexically enclosing scope. Objects that outlive the nesting go on the heap, which fragments and must be freed or collected.","one":"Each call gets a frame on a stack because calls nest, with one link to the caller and one to the enclosing scope.","gate":{"q":"Why does a single fixed storage area fail for a recursive procedure, and what does a stack provide instead?","a":"A recursive call needs its own copy of the parameters and locals while the outer copy stays alive. Stack allocation pushes an activation record per call and pops it on return, so calls nest and unwind in reverse order, each frame holding locals, saved status, a control link to the caller and an access link to the enclosing scope. Static allocation fixes one address per name and forbids recursion."},"keys":["Static allocation","Stack allocation","Heap allocation","Activation record","Control (dynamic) link","Access (static) link"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-57","q":"What is activation record? Discuss the different activities performed by caller and callee during procedure call and return.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-90","q":"List out the different types of runtime storage management techniques.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-21","q":"Draw and explain the complete structure of an activation record with all components (return value, actual parameters, control link, access link, saved machine status, local data, temporaries).","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5},{"id":"cdc-45","q":"How do you represent recursion in an activation tree? Generate the three-address code for the following instruction:","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Symbol Table Design","rank":9,"q":4,"m":25,"share":4,"cum":100,"hook":"The guest house book is asked the same question by reception, kitchen and laundry, and must answer all three the same way.","idea":"A symbol table is one shared map from identifier to attributes, with insert, lookup and scope-exit delete. A hash table gives near-constant access in practice; nesting is handled by a stack of tables, or one table with a scope field and an undo log, so an inner declaration shadows an outer one and disappears cleanly when its block ends.","one":"One shared register of names, indexed by hash and stacked by scope, so every phase gets the same answer cheaply.","gate":{"q":"Why does a symbol table use a hash index rather than a linear list, and what does the choice cost?","a":"A linear list answers every lookup by scanning all entries, so a file with n names costs quadratic time overall. A hash table maps the name to a bucket, giving constant average insert and lookup, and O(n) only when everything collides. The price is collision handling by chaining or open addressing, plus a stack of tables so an inner declaration shadows an outer one."},"keys":["Symbol table","Stored attributes","Operations","Linear list","Hash table","Hash functions"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-85","q":"What are the typical entries made in symbol table? Explain.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10},{"id":"cdc-20","q":"What is a symbol table? Compare different data structures for implementing symbol table:","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5},{"id":"cdc-41","q":"What types of information are stored in a symbol table? Discuss the activation record.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"cdc-6","q":"Explain the types of data structures used in a symbol table.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2082","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r26","q":"What is a symbol table? Compare different data structures for implementing symbol table:","count":2,"marks":5}]}]},"egov":{"name":"E-Governance","code":"CSC377","exam":"2026-12-16","totalQ":85,"totalM":530,"units":[{"n":2,"name":"Models of E-Governance","rank":1,"q":20,"m":140,"share":26,"cum":26,"hook":"Five models, five levels, but only two questions: which way does the information go, and who moves first?","idea":"The five models are the directions information can travel between state and citizen. Broadcasting pushes one way to everyone and hears nothing back; comparative analysis reuses the same published data as a ranking, so the comparison itself supplies pressure; critical flow aims the push at auditors and press; mobilisation reverses the arrow; interactive service closes it with a transaction the back office records. The five levels then measure who initiates and how far the transaction runs: the state publishes, the citizen asks, the citizen completes, and finally the state acts before being asked.","one":"Five models are five arrows; five levels are who moves first, the state, the citizen, or the system.","gate":{"q":"Which model of e-governance is a district ranking of schools, and why is it not broadcasting?","a":"Broadcasting pushes one way to everyone and hears nothing back, while comparative analysis reuses the same published data as a ranking, so the comparison itself supplies pressure. Critical flow aims the push at auditors and press; mobilisation reverses the arrow; interactive service closes it with a transaction the back office records."},"keys":["Broadcasting model","Critical flow model","Comparative analysis model","Mobilisation and lobbying","Interactive service (G2C2G)","Level 1, emerging"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-15","q":"Explain Mobilization and Lobbying Model and Government-to-Citizen-to-Government (G2C2G) Interactive Service Model in detail. How can these models be used to enhance citizen participation in governance, especially in the context of recent youth-led movements in Nepal?","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":10},{"id":"egov-2","q":"What is a digital governance model? Describe the Critical Flow Model in E-Governance. How does it differ from other models?","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"egov-26","q":"Why is it necessary to use E-Government model? Explain interactive service model and critical flow model in detail.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"egov-3","q":"What is the E-Governance maturity model? Explain the various maturity levels along with their characteristics.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r1","q":"What is the E-Governance maturity model? Explain the various maturity levels along with their characteristics.","count":3,"marks":10},{"id":"r3","q":"How can we achieve good governance through E-Government models? Explain.","count":3,"marks":5},{"id":"r15","q":"What is a digital governance model? Describe the Critical Flow Model in E-Governance. How does it differ from other models?","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":3,"name":"E-Government Infrastructure Development","rank":2,"q":19,"m":115,"share":22,"cum":48,"hook":"A ward gets a new water tank, and every tap in the village still runs dry by March.","idea":"Infrastructure is layered, and interoperability is the socket between the layers: network carries traffic, computing and the data centre run the applications, and the architecture layers sit above them. Each layer can only be as reliable as the one beneath it, so e-readiness is an honest audit of the weakest link. Without shared identifiers, schemas and APIs each ministry lays its own pipe, so the citizen becomes the messenger between offices.","one":"Infrastructure is a supply chain: a service can only be as good as the weakest layer under it.","gate":{"q":"Why can two ministries hold the same citizen and still be unable to exchange her data?","a":"Because infrastructure is layered and interoperability is the socket between layers. Network carries traffic, computing and the data centre run the applications, and the architecture layers sit above them. Without shared identifiers, schemas and APIs each ministry lays its own pipe, so the citizen becomes the messenger between offices."},"keys":["Network infrastructure","Computing infrastructure","Data centre","Six architecture layers","Interoperability framework","Cloud governance"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-14","q":"What is E-Government Architecture and Interoperability Framework? Why are these crucial for successful e-governance implementation? Explain different types of network infrastructure and computing infrastructure required with examples from Nepal’s e-governance projects.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":10},{"id":"egov-27","q":"What different infrastructure are necessary to implement E-governance? Explain different computing infrastructure necessary to implement E-governance.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"egov-51","q":"Why is it necessary to have IT infrastructure to implement E-Government? Explain different IT infrastructure needed to implement E-government.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"egov-63","q":"Why do we need IT infrastructure to implement E-Governance? Explain network and computing infrastructure needed to implement E-governance.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2078","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r19","q":"Define E-readiness. What is data system infrastructural preparedness and technological infrastructural preparedness?","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to E-Government and E-Governance","rank":3,"q":16,"m":115,"share":22,"cum":70,"hook":"A ward office paints its counter, adds a computer, and the same clerk still decides who waits.","idea":"E-government is ICT at the delivery edge, the output leg where a certificate leaves the building. E-governance is ICT across the whole loop: the inputs, the decision rule, the output, and the complaint that feeds back. That is why one is a subset of the other, and why a portal alone cannot fix a dishonest register.","one":"E-government moves the counter online; e-governance puts the register, the budget and the complaint box there too.","gate":{"q":"Why is an online certificate counter e-government but not e-governance?","a":"E-government is ICT at the delivery edge, where a certificate leaves the building. E-governance covers the whole loop: citizen data in, the decision rule, the output, and the complaint feeding back. A portal therefore speeds the queue while the register stays closed, so speed rises and trust does not."},"keys":["E-Government","E-Governance","G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E","Information-system view","Electronic vs online delivery","E-government life cycle"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-1","q":"What are the present global trends of growth in E-Governance? Discuss with examples from developed and developing nations.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"egov-13","q":"What is E-Government Life Cycle? Explain each stage in detail. How can E-Governance help bridge the digital divide and ensure inclusive service delivery in Nepal’s diverse geographical and socio-economic context? Explain with suitable examples.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":10},{"id":"egov-25","q":"What are the benefits of using E-governance for both government and citizens? Why is it necessary to implement E-governance to provide government services to citizens efficiently? Explain with suitable example.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"egov-37","q":"Differentiate between e-governance and e-government. Explain the issues in e-governance implementations and how to solve these issues?","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":10}],"reps":[{"id":"r12","q":"What are the benefits of using E-governance for both government and citizens? Why is it necessary to implement E-governance to provide government services to citizens efficiently? Explain with suitable example.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r13","q":"What are the present global trends of growth in E-Governance? Discuss with examples from developed and developing nations.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":4,"name":"Security for E-Government","rank":4,"q":11,"m":55,"share":10,"cum":80,"hook":"A thief who steals cash is caught by the missing cash; a thief who edits a land record leaves everything looking correct.","idea":"Government security protects authenticity and attribution as much as secrecy, because a forged state record is worth more than the bribe that buys it. Controls therefore stack: physical, network, host, application, data, identity, audit, people. Each layer assumes the one above has already failed, and the audit trail is what turns a silent edit into a caught one.","one":"Government security guards the state's word, not its cash: the attacker wants a record that looks true.","gate":{"q":"Why does a stolen government database differ from a stolen cash box?","a":"The cash box loses value that is visibly missing, while the database can be edited so that everything still balances. Government therefore protects authenticity and attribution, not only secrecy. Controls stack physical, network, host, application, data, identity, audit and people, each assuming the layer above failed, and append-only logs turn a silent edit into a caught one."},"keys":["Defence in depth","Security management model","Physical layer","Network layer","Application layer","Data layer"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-19","q":"Explain the Security Management Model for e-government systems.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"egov-24","q":"Discuss the significance of cyber laws in protecting government digital infrastructure and citizen data in Nepal.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"egov-33","q":"What is E-governance security architecture? Explain.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"egov-35","q":"What is cyber law? How can we use this law to protect government data?","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Case Studies","rank":5,"q":11,"m":55,"share":10,"cum":91,"hook":"A mechanic keeps case files so a new noise is matched to an old fault, not to a town.","idea":"A case is evidence for one fault class, not a country report. Bhoomi put the record of rights online and printed it at kiosks, so the village official's power to withhold or alter it disappeared; the measured gain was days and bribes, and the limitation was legal validity and connectivity. Read every case on four axes — fault, intervention, evidence, limitation — and the list stops being history: naming those four is what transfers to an unseen case.","one":"A case study is a diagnosis with a budget: pick it for the fault it demonstrates, not the country it happened in.","gate":{"q":"Why does Bhoomi earn marks as a removed middleman rather than as a Karnataka land portal?","a":"Because a case is evidence for one fault class, not a country report. Bhoomi put the record of rights online and printed it at kiosks, so the village official's power to withhold or alter it disappeared; the measured gain was days and bribes, and the limitation was legal validity and connectivity. Naming fault, intervention, evidence and limitation is what transfers to an unseen case."},"keys":["Bhoomi","e-Seva","Smart Nagarpalika","Nagarik App","NIC and NICNET","CARD"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-11","q":"Describe any two E-Government initiatives in Nepal and their impact.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":5},{"id":"egov-21","q":"Discuss the role of Smart Nagarpalika initiative in promoting e-governance at the local level in Nepal. What lessons can Nepal learn from the Bhoomi project in land records management?","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"egov-44","q":"Mention the existing scenario of e-governance in Nepal.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":5},{"id":"egov-47","q":"Explain in brief about e-governance initiatives in Srilanka.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r25","q":"What are the main contents are needed for human resource management software? Explain.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":5,"name":"Data Warehousing and Data Mining in Government","rank":6,"q":8,"m":50,"share":9,"cum":100,"hook":"A cooperative's day book records today's sale; the closed ledger decides whether the year can be trusted.","idea":"A warehouse is the closed ledger: subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile. ETL fixes the definitions once at load, so every report counts the same thing. Mining is then disciplined questioning of stable history, finding patterns a till can never show, because the till overwrites yesterday in order to record today. The operational system is current, normalised and volatile, tuned for many small transactions rather than few large queries, so each correction overwrites yesterday; the warehouse fixes definitions once, freezes each period, and serves marts and OLAP, so mining can find patterns the live register cannot.","one":"A warehouse is the closed ledger behind the till: fixed definitions, frozen history, and patterns the till cannot see.","gate":{"q":"Why can a live land-records database not answer how enrolment changed over ten years?","a":"Because the operational system is current, normalised and volatile, tuned for many small transactions rather than few large historical queries. Each correction overwrites yesterday, and each ministry applies its own definition. A warehouse extracts through ETL, fixes definitions once, freezes each period, and serves marts and OLAP, so mining can find patterns the live register cannot."},"keys":["Data warehouse","Four characteristics","ETL","Data mart","OLTP versus OLAP","Star schema"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-38","q":"What do you mean by data warehousing and data mining? Explain the applications of data warehousing and data mining in census and prices of essential commodities.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":10},{"id":"egov-75","q":"Explain the components of data warehouse. What are the applications of data mining in eGovernance in the context of Nepal.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2076","marks":10},{"id":"egov-20","q":"How can data warehousing and data mining be utilized in planning, commerce, and rural development sectors? Explain with examples from Nepal.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"egov-34","q":"Discuss how data warehousing and data mining is used in census data.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r14","q":"What do you mean by data warehousing and data mining? Explain the applications of data warehousing and data mining in census and prices of essential commodities.","count":2,"marks":10}]}]},"ncc":{"name":".NET Centric Computing","code":"CSC378","exam":"2026-12-20","totalQ":80,"totalM":490,"units":[{"n":1,"name":"Language Preliminaries","rank":1,"q":17,"m":110,"share":22,"cum":22,"hook":"Why can the same method call print two different names, depending on how the variable was declared?","idea":"C# is compiled twice. Roslyn turns source into MSIL plus metadata inside an assembly, and the CLR JIT-compiles each method to native code on its first call, then caches it. Value types copy their data inline; reference types hold a heap reference. A virtual call with override resolves by the object's real type at run time, while new binds by the declared type at compile time.","one":"C# compiles once to a portable form, then to native code per machine; only virtual calls wait to see the real object.","gate":{"q":"Why can the same method call execute different code depending on how the variable was declared?","a":"Because binding, not the object, decides which body runs. A virtual method reached through override is resolved at run time from the object's actual type in its method table, so the derived body executes. A non-virtual method, or a base method hidden with new, is bound to the declared type at compile time, so the base body runs even when the object is derived."},"keys":["MSIL (CIL)","CLI","CLR","JIT compilation","Value vs reference type","override vs new"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-1","q":"Define lambda expression. Distinguish between expression lambda and statement lambda. Write a program for asynchronous file read. List the benefits of Async and Await.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-13","q":"What is the difference between abstract class, interface, and sealed class? When would you use each? Write a program demonstrating the use of abstract class with abstract methods and sealed class to prevent inheritance.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-27","q":"Distinguish between collection and generics. What are named and positional attribute parameters? Write a program to create your own exception when the user gives subject name than “C#”.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-41","q":"What are the needs for partial class and sealed class? How do you relate delegate with events? State the steps to depl0y .NET core application.","y":"NET Centric Question 2080","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":4,"name":"Creating ASP.NET Core MVC Applications","rank":2,"q":11,"m":70,"share":14,"cum":37,"hook":"A form arrives with three fields missing. Who was supposed to notice, the browser or the server?","idea":"Routing maps a URL to a controller action, model binding fills action parameters, and data annotations decide whether ModelState is valid before your code runs. The action then chooses an action result: View renders Razor, Json returns data, RedirectToAction sends the browser elsewhere. That redirect exists because the browser's last request must not be a POST: returning the view leaves the address bar on the submitted URL, so refresh or back repeats the form and inserts a duplicate row, while a 302 and a GET leave an idempotent request that can be repeated harmlessly.","one":"An action is a route, a bound model, a validity check and a chosen result, with its collaborators injected rather than built.","gate":{"q":"Why does a successful POST action return RedirectToAction instead of View?","a":"Because the browser's last request must not be a POST. Returning the view leaves the address bar on the submitted URL, so refresh or back repeats the form and inserts a duplicate row. RedirectToAction answers with a 302 and a GET to a safe URL, leaving an idempotent request that can be repeated harmlessly."},"keys":["Controller","IActionResult","Action results","Conventional routing","Attribute routing","Model binding sources"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-14","q":"What are different Action Result Types in ASP.NET Core MVC? Explain with examples. Design an API Controller that returns JSON response for a Product entity (ProductId, Name, Price, Category) with GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE operations.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-2","q":"What is the process to capture Razor output? Explain the ASP.NET Core features to dynamically generate HTML content using C# and send it to the client, separating the application’s concern.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-28","q":"How do you create controller? Illustrate with an example. Describe the procedure of rendering HTML with Razor with scenario.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-19","q":"What is model binding in ASP.NET Core? Explain server-side model validation with data annotations.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":8,"name":"Securing ASP.NET Core","rank":3,"q":10,"m":55,"share":11,"cum":48,"hook":"A user is logged in and still gets a 403. What is the difference between that and a 401?","idea":"Authentication establishes identity: Identity hashes passwords with PBKDF2, stores users, roles and claims, and issues a cookie or token. Authorization then decides access through [Authorize], roles, policies or claims, and runs after authentication in the pipeline. Anti-forgery pairs a cookie with a form token. Razor encodes output, parameters keep data out of SQL syntax, and Url.IsLocalUrl blocks an off-site returnUrl. That order is not optional: authorization can only judge a principal that already exists, so reversed it would meet an anonymous principal, every protected action would challenge with 401, and no role check could ever pass.","one":"Authentication says who you are, authorization says which doors open, and every attack is the server trusting the client once too often.","gate":{"q":"Why does ASP.NET Core run authentication before authorization in the pipeline?","a":"Because authorization can only judge a principal that already exists. Authentication reads the cookie or token and fills HttpContext.User with claims, then every Authorize attribute, role and policy is evaluated against those claims. Reversed, authorization would meet an anonymous principal, so every protected action would challenge with 401 and no role check could ever pass."},"keys":["Authentication","Authorization","ASP.NET Core Identity","Password hashing","Claims","Policies"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-58","q":"Explain the uses of cookies. What types of vulnerabilities can occur in web application? Show the case of Cross-site Scripting attack.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-12","q":"What is SPA? Write code to define a role for admin who can create, update, and delete any article.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-24","q":"What is ASP.NET Core Identity? Explain the process of adding authentication to an application and configuring identity services.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-34","q":"Distinguish between roles and policies. Explain about Kestrel web server in ASP.NET core.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Working with Database","rank":4,"q":8,"m":55,"share":11,"cum":59,"hook":"Two programs read the same table; one breaks when a column is renamed and the other does not. Why?","idea":"ADO.NET is the hand translator: SqlConnection opens, SqlCommand carries SQL and parameters, ExecuteReader streams rows forward-only, and an adapter fills a disconnected DataSet. EF Core is the clerk: a DbSet is a table, LINQ is the request, and the DbContext tracks each loaded entity so SaveChanges writes only the differences. Either way parameters keep code and data on separate channels: the statement text is parsed and its plan built before any value is bound, so a value can only ever occupy a slot and never become syntax. Manual escaping or stripping is a blocklist that misses numeric contexts, second-order data and encoding differences, so one forgotten path still exposes the table.","one":"ADO.NET makes you write the SQL, while EF Core writes it from LINQ and remembers what you changed until SaveChanges.","gate":{"q":"Why do parameters stop SQL injection when escaping quotes in the input does not?","a":"Because parameters keep code and data on separate channels. The statement text is parsed and its plan built before any value is bound, so a value can only ever occupy a slot and never become syntax. Manual escaping or stripping is a blocklist that misses numeric contexts, second-order data and encoding differences, so one forgotten path still exposes the table."},"keys":["SqlConnection","SqlCommand","ExecuteNonQuery","ExecuteScalar","SqlDataReader","SqlDataAdapter and DataSet"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-29","q":"List some advantages of Entity Framework over ADO.NET. Assume a database named “IOST” with a table FACULTY(Course_Name, CourseID, No_of_Semester, fee). Insert some records using ADO.NET and retrive the fee of records having name CSIT.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-3","q":"Why do we need ORM? List the components of ADO.NET. Assume a database with a relation BOOK(ID, NAME, PRICE). Write a function to fetch a list of books having price more than 1000 using ADO.NET.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-39","q":"How do you cache the information of the client and what are its uses? Create a table named BAG(ID, Brand, Price, Date). Perform the following operations using Entity Framework Core.","y":"NET Centric Question 2080","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-20","q":"Explain the role of Connection, Command, Reader, and Adapter classes in ADO.NET. How do they work together to perform database operations?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"State Management","rank":5,"q":8,"m":45,"share":9,"cum":68,"hook":"You log in, click one link, and the site asks you to log in again. What was never stored?","idea":"Cookies live in the browser and travel with every request, so they are small, visible and tamperable unless protected. Session keeps the data on the server and sends only an id cookie, so it escapes the size limit but needs a store. TempData carries one message across one redirect and is read once. ViewData lasts a single request; IMemoryCache is shared by all users until it expires or is removed.","one":"State is the memory HTTP refuses to keep, so you choose an owner, a lifetime and a trust boundary for every piece of it.","gate":{"q":"Why does a shopping cart survive a page reload when HTTP itself keeps no memory of the visitor?","a":"Because the cart is not in the protocol, it is in a store the server or browser owns. Session keeps the cart server-side and sends only an opaque id cookie, which every request carries back so middleware can reattach the right dictionary, while a database row keyed to the user does the same. Clear the cookie or let it expire and the cart is gone."},"keys":["Stateless HTTP","Session","TempData","Peek and Keep","ViewData and ViewBag","HttpContext.Items"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-40","q":"How do you manage state on stateless HTTP? How do you validate form using JQuery? Describe with your own assumptions.","y":"NET Centric Question 2080","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-10","q":"What is the use of a partial class? Discuss session state.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-21","q":"What is TempData? How is it different from Session state and ViewData? When would you use TempData over other state management techniques","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-22","q":"Explain the use of Cache as a server-side state management strategy. What are the benefits of caching in web applications?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":2,"name":"Introduction to ASP.NET","rank":6,"q":7,"m":45,"share":9,"cum":78,"hook":"Why does the same web application need a different installer on Windows, on Linux and on a phone?","idea":".NET Framework is the Windows-only runtime the old web stack assumed. .NET Core and .NET 5+ are cross-platform, open source and modular: the runtime and libraries arrive as NuGet packages with the application, so versions sit side by side. Mono is a separate implementation for other platforms, .NET Standard a contract of shared APIs, and ASP.NET Core one chassis carrying MVC, Web API, Razor Pages and SignalR. Because the runtime is resolved per application rather than installed once for the machine, each project restores its framework and the host loads it inside that application's own process, where a machine-wide framework would force every site onto one version and one update cycle.","one":"ASP.NET Core is a cross-platform runtime plus per-project packages, not a Windows feature, and one chassis carries every web style.","gate":{"q":"Why can two ASP.NET Core applications that need different runtime versions run side by side on one server?","a":"Because the runtime is resolved per application rather than installed once for the machine. Each project restores the framework as NuGet packages or names the shared runtime version in its csproj, and the host loads it inside that application's own process. A machine-wide framework would force every site on the server onto one version and one update cycle."},"keys":[".NET Framework",".NET Core / .NET 5+","Mono",".NET Standard","Web Forms","ASP.NET MVC"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-15","q":"Differentiate between .NET, .NET Core, and Mono frameworks. Explain the compilation and execution process of .NET applications covering CLI, MSIL, and CLR. Discuss different hosting models for ASP.NET Core applications: IIS, Nginx, Kestrel, and Docker containerization.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-57","q":"What is the purpose of web form? Write a program to demonstrate the concept of collection and generics.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-5","q":"What are the logics behind the .NET architecture and design principles? Explain.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-51","q":"Answer the following questions in short.","y":"NET Centric Question 2076","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Hosting and Deployment","rank":7,"q":7,"m":45,"share":9,"cum":87,"hook":"The application runs on your laptop and dies on the server: same code, different front door.","idea":"Kestrel is the cross-platform web server that owns the socket; IIS, Nginx or Apache usually sit in front as a reverse proxy for TLS, compression and load balancing. In-process hosting runs the app inside the IIS worker process, while out-of-process has the ASP.NET Core Module forward requests to a separate Kestrel. A multi-stage Dockerfile compiles on the SDK image and copies only the published output onto the smaller runtime image. Because the proxy forwards rather than executes, it terminates TLS and passes plain HTTP to a Kestrel process listening on localhost, so forwarded headers must be honoured and TLS must be terminated in exactly one place.","one":"Deployment is deciding who owns the socket and where configuration lives: Kestrel serves, a proxy fronts it, a container carries it.","gate":{"q":"Why must an ASP.NET Core application keep Kestrel even when IIS or Nginx sits in front of it?","a":"Because the proxy forwards rather than executes. IIS through the ASP.NET Core Module, and Nginx on Linux, terminate TLS and pass plain HTTP to a Kestrel process listening on localhost, which is the only server that understands the application. That is why forwarded headers must be honoured and TLS must be terminated in exactly one place."},"keys":["Kestrel","In-process hosting","Out-of-process hosting","ANCM","Nginx","Dockerfile"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-59","q":"Describe any two hosting models for core application. Design a client side validation application using jquery for taking input data from student in Library Management System, assume your own assumptions.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-70","q":"Explain the procedure for building, running, and deploying .NET core applications. Write a program to show the possibility of SQL injection attack.","y":"NET Centric Question 2078","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-11","q":"Explain the core differences between a traditional Virtual Machine (VM) and a Docker container.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-25","q":"What is the ASP.NET Core Module? How does it work with IIS to host ASP.NET Core applications?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r10","q":"Explain the procedure for building, running, and deploying .NET core applications. Write a program to show the possibility of SQL injection attack.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":3,"name":"HTTP and ASP.NET Core","rank":8,"q":6,"m":35,"share":7,"cum":94,"hook":"Every request enters by the same door, yet a stylesheet and a login page are handled by different guards.","idea":"Kestrel accepts the connection and parses one HTTP message: method, path, headers, optional body. It passes that into a middleware pipeline assembled in Program.cs, where each component may act, pass on, or stop and write a response. Order is the contract: exception handling, HTTPS, static files, routing, authentication, authorization, endpoints. The controller works with the model and returns a view, and the response retraces the same layers.","one":"A request is one HTTP message walked through an ordered pipeline, and the first layer that answers ends the journey.","gate":{"q":"Why does a request for a stylesheet never reach the controller while a request for a protected page does?","a":"Because both enter the same pipeline but stop at different layers. Static file middleware recognises the extension, writes the file and short-circuits, so routing, authentication and the action never run. A page URL matches no file, falls through to routing and the endpoint, so authentication and authorization must approve it before the action executes."},"keys":["HTTP","Request line","Status codes","Idempotent methods","Kestrel","Middleware pipeline"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-71","q":"What are the advantages of stateless HTTP in ASP.NET core application? Create a method to insert a record (3, “John , 12000”) onto the table Employee having fields Employeeld(int), Name varchar(200), Salary(int) using Entity Framework.","y":"NET Centric Question 2078","marks":10},{"id":"ncc-18","q":"Describe the ASP.NET Core architecture overview. How does it differ from traditional ASP.NET framework architecture?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-31","q":"How does ASP.NET core MVC provides patterns-based way to build dynamic website? Explain.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-6","q":"Describe any four ways to read HTTP response in ASP.NET Core.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Client-side Development","rank":9,"q":6,"m":30,"share":6,"cum":100,"hook":"The form was validated before it was sent, and the server still rejected it. Which side was lying?","idea":"Client-side code runs in the browser and can be read, skipped or replaced, so it buys speed, never authority: the browser is a runtime the visitor controls and cannot grant authority. The script is downloaded, readable and disposable, so a direct request, a disabled script or an edited form reaches the action with any values at all. ModelState re-runs the same annotations server-side before the database is touched, which is why client rules only save round trips. jQuery with unobtrusive validation mirrors those rules for instant feedback, AJAX exchanges JSON without a reload, and a SPA renders and routes in the browser and calls a Web API.","one":"The browser is a second runtime that may be fast but must never be trusted; every check it does, the server repeats.","gate":{"q":"Why must the server repeat a validation rule the browser has already enforced?","a":"Because the browser is a runtime the visitor controls and cannot grant authority. The script is downloaded, readable and disposable, so a direct request, a disabled script or an edited form reaches the action with any values at all. ModelState re-runs the same annotations server-side before the database is touched, which is why client rules only save round trips."},"keys":["Unobtrusive validation","jQuery AJAX","_ValidationScriptsPartial","SPA","Angular","React"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-23","q":"Compare Angular and React frameworks for building Single Page Applications (SPA) with ASP.NET Core backend. What are the advantages and disadvantages of each?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-33","q":"Write a program to validate the form that takes input user name, password and phone number using JQuery. All fields must be non-empty and phone number must contain exactly 10 digits.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-56","q":"a. Explain the client-side scripting with practical example.","y":"NET Centric Question 2076","marks":5},{"id":"ncc-62","q":"What does single page application means? Discuss about server side strategies.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":5}],"reps":[]}]},"tw":{"name":"Technical Writing","code":"CSC379","exam":"2026-12-23","totalQ":95,"totalM":665,"units":[{"n":1,"name":"What Is Technical Writing","rank":1,"q":13,"m":95,"share":14,"cum":14,"hook":"A water pump bought in Asan comes with a glossy brochure, and a folded sheet with a diagram and three warnings.","idea":"Technical writing is writing that changes what a specific reader can do, so its success test is behavioural, not aesthetic. Its characteristics follow from that: clarity removes ambiguity about who does what, accuracy keeps a verified number usable, and organisation lets a scanning reader reach the step. The consequence is measurable, since a correct installation with no support call proves the document worked; creative writing is judged by how it makes you feel, technical writing by whether the task gets done.","one":"Technical writing is judged by what the reader can do afterwards, not by how good the prose sounds.","gate":{"q":"Why is technical writing judged by what the reader can do afterwards rather than by how the prose sounds?","a":"Technical writing exists to change an action, so its success test is behavioural, not aesthetic. Its characteristics follow from that: clarity removes ambiguity about who does what, accuracy keeps a verified number usable, and organisation lets a scanning reader reach the step. The consequence is measurable, since a correct installation with no support call proves the document worked."},"keys":["Technical writing","Clarity","Conciseness","Accuracy","Audience awareness","Objectivity"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-66","q":"Write in detail on advantages and disadvantages using Facebook, Twitter and google+ for communication. In your write up use these expressions! in brief, whereas, however, though, even though, at first, in general, finally.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"tw-68","q":"Define “simple sentence”, “complex sentence” and “compound sentence”. Give three examples for each type.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"tw-69","q":"What’s technical communication? How does it differ from other types of communication? What features help making communication ‘technical’?","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"tw-70","q":"What skills do you need to improve your technical writing? How can you acquire those","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"The Writing Process","rank":2,"q":12,"m":85,"share":13,"cum":27,"hook":"A mason builds a boundary wall in one pass and finds it leaning at the far end, with the cement already set.","idea":"The process separates thinking from wording from polishing, because each needs a different kind of attention and doing all three at once does none well. Planning decides what the document is, drafting produces material without judging it, revising judges structure and content, copyediting fixes the surface, publishing puts it in the reader's hands. It loops, because a revision can send you back to planning.","one":"Draft fast, revise the structure, then polish the words — never all three at the same time.","gate":{"q":"Why does the writing process place revision before copyediting instead of polishing as you draft?","a":"Each stage needs a different attention, and mixing them does none well. Drafting without judging keeps pace and exposes missing structure, revising then tests content, order and completeness against the purpose, and copyediting fixes grammar and consistency only after the structure has stopped moving. Polishing a section that should be cut is wasted work, so the order protects the deadline."},"keys":["Planning","Drafting","Revising","Copyediting","Proofreading","Publishing"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-14","q":"Explain the complete technical writing process from planning to publishing. How does collaborative writing enhance or complicate this process? Discuss the advantages, challenges, and conflict resolution strategies in collaborative writing. Support your answer with examples from software documentation or IT project scenarios.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10},{"id":"tw-2","q":"Discuss the writing process for technical documents. Illustrate each stage with relevant examples from IT workplace writing.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"tw-44","q":"As writing is not an overnight endeavor it follows a certain process. Discuss a process for technical writing.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10},{"id":"tw-71","q":"How important is a collaborative effort in preparing documents? Discuss its advantages","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Information Reports","rank":3,"q":13,"m":75,"share":11,"cum":38,"hook":"The health post sends the district office a monthly sheet of numbers, with no opinion attached.","idea":"An information report carries facts to a reader who was not present, in a shape that reader can use. Nothing is recommended, because the recommendation belongs to whoever has the authority. If the writer does recommend, the report claims authority it was not given, so it is either rejected or acted on as advice it was never scoped to give. Structure follows the reader's questions: what period, what happened, what is unfinished, what changed the plan. The summary is the plain-language version for the manager; the abstract is the compressed technical version for the specialist.","one":"An information report carries facts, not advice, in a shape that lets an absent reader compare and decide.","gate":{"q":"Why does an information report lose its definition the moment it says we recommend?","a":"An information report presents findings so that whoever holds authority can decide, while a recommendation report performs that deciding. The moment the writer writes we recommend, the document claims authority it was not given, and the reader can no longer separate evidence from judgement. The consequence is that the report is either rejected or acted on as an advisory document it was never scoped to be."},"keys":["Information report","Summary","Abstract","Informative abstract","Descriptive abstract","Mechanism description"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-30","q":"Describe the structure and key components of a technical report. How does this structure enhance the report’s effectiveness?","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"tw-77","q":"Write news release for your college to announce an education fair.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"tw-23","q":"Your company has developed a new cybersecurity software product. Write a persuasive news release (maximum 300 words) announcing this launch suitable for tech media outlets. Use the inverted pyramid structure.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5},{"id":"tw-35","q":"What is the purpose of an executive summary in a technical document?","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":4,"name":"Brief Correspondence","rank":4,"q":10,"m":70,"share":11,"cum":49,"hook":"An email with the subject line 'Query' sits unopened in a bank manager's inbox behind forty others.","idea":"Correspondence is built for a reader who is still deciding whether to give you their attention: the subject names the business, the first line states the purpose, the middle carries only what is needed to decide or act, and the close says who does what next. The distance decides the ceremony. A memo stays inside one organisation, so it needs no salutation, no inside address and no complimentary close, only TO, FROM, DATE and SUBJECT; a letter may travel to a customer, supplier or authority, so it must identify both parties, carry a signature and survive being filed.","one":"Every message is a knock on a busy person's door: say who you are, what you want, and by when.","gate":{"q":"Why is a memo structured differently from a business letter?","a":"A memo stays inside one organisation, so it needs no salutation, no inside address and no complimentary close, only TO, FROM, DATE and SUBJECT above the body. A letter may travel to a customer, supplier or authority, so it must identify both parties, carry a signature and survive being filed. The distance decides the ceremony."},"keys":["Memo","Business letter parts","Full block format","Modified block","Direct plan","Indirect plan"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-29","q":"You are now studying IT in a reputed campus under Tribhuvan University, Nepal. The campus has a well-equipped computer lab. You remember the school where you completed your secondary-level education. Most schools in Nepal have only a couple of desktop computers. You want to donate about a dozen computers to the school by raising funds. Write a letter to the Chief District Officer requesting him/her to permit you to collect donations. Include the specifications of the computers and the price list with the letter.","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"tw-64","q":"Write a business letter to a Chinese computer company, Beijing for regular supply of assembled computers and computers parts as you are running a computer shop at New Road, Kathmandu. Include the issues or modality of payment and possible problems in business transaction.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"tw-82","q":"Write an e-mail that announces a new skill-oriented program at Shikhar IT Solutions. The program will allow workers to share each other’s experience and grow together.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"tw-83","q":"The Board of Directors of your company Nepal Distillery Ltd. has passed a proposal on providing bonus and incentives to all the employees. As a secretary of the company, you are responsible for informing the staff about the decision taken by the board meeting. Write a memo informing about the recently taken decision.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Document Design and Graphics","rank":5,"q":10,"m":65,"share":10,"cum":59,"hook":"A patient at the hospital gate cannot read the board, and still finds the X-ray room in ten seconds.","idea":"Design is signposting for a reader under pressure, who goes to the figure first and may never read the paragraph that explains it. Proximity groups what belongs together, alignment puts things on a shared line, repetition teaches the reader your pattern once, and contrast says what matters first. A graphic needs titles, axis labels with units, a legend and a source so it can be understood in isolation, and those also stop a truncated axis from exaggerating a difference; without them the reader guesses, and a guess in a procedure or a budget is a defect.","one":"Design for the reader who scans: group, align, repeat, contrast, and label every graphic so it stands alone.","gate":{"q":"Why does a chart need a title, labelled axes with units, a legend and a source?","a":"A reader under pressure goes to the figure first and may never read the paragraph that explains it. Titles, axis labels with units, a legend and a source let the graphic be understood in isolation, and they also stop a truncated axis from exaggerating a difference. Without them the reader guesses, and a guess in a procedure or a budget is a defect."},"keys":["CRAP principles","White space","Serif vs sans","Left-ragged alignment","Line graph","Bar chart"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-3","q":"What role do document design principles play in technical communication? Explain with examples.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"tw-46","q":"What are graphics? How are they used in technical writing?","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10},{"id":"tw-67","q":"Explain the importance of using table, illustrations, graphic presentation in an academic writing. Give some suitable examples.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10},{"id":"tw-11","q":"Make a flow-chart diagram of your dream application.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r24","q":"Make a flow-chart diagram of your dream application.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":2,"name":"Audience and Purpose","rank":6,"q":7,"m":60,"share":9,"cum":68,"hook":"One notice about road widening is pinned at the ward office, and five people read it needing five different things.","idea":"Audience analysis is deciding which reader the document is built around. The primary reader acts on it, secondary readers approve or pass it on, tertiary readers meet it by accident. Their knowledge, role, attitude and where they read it decide your vocabulary, your depth and your order of information. Purpose is the single verb the document must accomplish.","one":"Write for the one reader who must act, then add what the others need without rewriting it for them.","gate":{"q":"Why must a writer name one primary reader before deciding vocabulary, depth and medium?","a":"The primary reader is the person who must act, so their knowledge and situation set the ceiling on every choice. If the document is built around that reader, vocabulary matches what they know, depth stops where their task ends, and the medium fits where they will read it. A document aimed at everyone drifts toward the average and fails the one reader who must act."},"keys":["Primary audience","Secondary audience","Tertiary audience","Knowledge level","Attitude","Reader persona"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-1","q":"Explain the concept of audience analysis in technical writing. Develop an audience profile for a user manual of a mobile banking application.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"tw-28","q":"Explain the role of audience analysis in technical writing and provide an example of how understanding the audience can affect the content and style of a technical document.","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"tw-45","q":"You have watched one of your peer’s vlog (video blog) entries on the issue of “Blocked mind as a malfunction of communication”. What have you learned in addition to your ideas you learned from your teacher? How do your experiences in unblocking the mind differ from your peer? Write feedback to your peer’s vlog.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10},{"id":"tw-65","q":"Write a newspaper article on “use of computer in Higher education” using headings subheadings, you should write it thinking about the aim and audience of your writing.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Writing for the Web","rank":7,"q":8,"m":55,"share":8,"cum":76,"hook":"A bus stops for forty seconds at a highway tea shop, and the shop has forty seconds to be understood.","idea":"Web readers scan for relevance before they commit to reading, so the page answers at the top and expands downwards. The inverted pyramid puts the conclusion first, chunking splits it into one-idea blocks, and headings, bullets and front-loaded keywords make those blocks findable. One page does one task, because a page that does three gives the visitor three chances to leave. The visitor arrives from a search result, mid-task, and leaves at no cost, so attention must be earned in the first screen, and a page that builds to its answer spends the reader's patience before delivering anything.","one":"On the web you have seconds: put the answer first, one idea per chunk, and make every heading scannable.","gate":{"q":"Why must a web page state its answer before its background?","a":"The visitor arrives from a search result, mid-task, and leaves at no cost, so attention must be earned in the first screen. The inverted pyramid therefore puts the conclusion first, then supporting detail, then background. A page that builds to its answer spends the reader's patience before delivering anything, and the visit ends without the answer being read."},"keys":["Inverted pyramid","Chunking","Scannability","Descriptive link text","Second person","One page, one task"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-15","q":"You are working as a web content manager for a software development company in Nepal. Your company wants to redesign its website to improve user experience, accessibility, and search engine visibility. Write a comprehensive proposal addressing: (a) Key principles for organizing and designing web pages, (b) Best practices for writing effective web content, (c) Information architecture considerations, (d) How to ensure web accessibility for users with disabilities. Include specific examples relevant to a tech company.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10},{"id":"tw-75","q":"Write a FAQ on how to get admission to B.Sc. CSIT at TU.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"tw-86","q":"What should one keep in mind when writing for the web? Discuss.","y":"TW Question Bank 2078","marks":10},{"id":"tw-12","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":8,"name":"Employment Communication","rank":8,"q":7,"m":45,"share":7,"cum":83,"hook":"Two hundred resumes reach a software company for one post, and the shortlist is made in an afternoon.","idea":"Employment writing is an argument addressed to one advertised job. The resume supplies evidence, arranged in whichever order makes the strongest case — time, skill, or both — and every line answers the reader's only question. The cover letter supplies the reason for this employer in particular. Neither is a record of a life; both are a match between a stated need and demonstrated work.","one":"A resume is an argument that you can do this job, evidenced in numbers, not an autobiography.","gate":{"q":"Why is a résumé described as an argument rather than a record of your career?","a":"A résumé is read against one advertised post, by a reader who has twenty seconds and one question: can this person do this job. So it selects evidence rather than listing duties, orders it by whatever makes the strongest case, and quantifies results. A career record explains what happened to you, while a targeted argument answers the employer's need."},"keys":["Résumé","CV","Chronological résumé","Functional résumé","Combination résumé","Achievement statement"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-76","q":"If you are seeking a summer or part-time job, applying for admission to a school, or","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"tw-78","q":"Suppose you have just completed the undergraduate degree (with a high GPA). Create a","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"tw-21","q":"Compare and contrast chronological resumes with functional resumes. When would you recommend using each type? Create a brief functional resume highlighting skills for a Technical Writer position.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5},{"id":"tw-37","q":"Differentiate chronological resumes from functional resumes.","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":12,"name":"Ethics in the Workplace","rank":9,"q":6,"m":40,"share":6,"cum":89,"hook":"The Dashain bonus memo is due at five, and three contract staff are not on the list.","idea":"Ethics is what you do where the rulebook stops and someone still has to choose. Accuracy is a property of each sentence, while honesty is a property of the whole document: omitting three contract staff from a bonus memo, starting a chart axis at ninety per cent, or moving a bad month to an appendix leaves every figure correct yet steers the reader to a false conclusion. The test is whether a reader holding only this document would reach the conclusion the writer reached.","one":"Ethics in writing is whether a reader with only your document would reach the same conclusion you did.","gate":{"q":"Why is a factually accurate document still capable of misleading its reader?","a":"Accuracy is a property of each sentence, while honesty is a property of the whole document. Omitting three contract staff from a bonus memo, starting a chart axis at ninety per cent, or moving a bad month to an appendix leaves every figure correct yet steers the reader to a false conclusion. The test is whether a reader holding only this document would reach the conclusion the writer reached."},"keys":["Ethics","Ethical dilemma","Conflict of interest","Whistleblowing","Plagiarism","Culture of ethics"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-16","q":"Discuss the importance of ethics in technical writing and workplace communication. What ethical dilemmas might technical writers face in the IT industry? Explaxain the steps a technical writer should take when confronted with ethical issues such as inaccurate information, plagiarism, or organizational wrongdoing. How can organizations create a culture of ethics? Support your answer with real-world examples.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10},{"id":"tw-31","q":"What are the ethical considerations in technical writing, and how can a writer ensure they adhere to ethical standards?","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"tw-25","q":"You discovered that your company’s technical documentation contains inaccurate safety information about a software vulnerability that could potentially harm users. Write a brief memo to your supervisor explaining the situation and recommending immediate corrective action.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5},{"id":"tw-27","q":"Write short notes on: a) Whistleblowing in technical communication b) Mechanism description versus process description","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Presentations","rank":10,"q":5,"m":35,"share":5,"cum":94,"hook":"A bus to Pokhara has eleven stops, and the driver who stops everywhere arrives at midnight.","idea":"A presentation is a queue of one idea per slide, held together by signposting. The introduction buys attention and sets the route, the body delivers one point at a time with a spoken transition between, and the conclusion repeats the point and asks for the action. Slides carry evidence, not script, because an audience can read or listen but not both. An audience holds about one idea at a time, so eleven half-ideas leave the room with none, and a slide of paragraph text is read instead of heard.","one":"One idea per slide, signposted joins, rehearsed to time — an audience can hold one thing at a time.","gate":{"q":"Why does a presentation fail when the speaker tries to say everything the report contains?","a":"An audience holds about one idea at a time, so a talk that carries eleven half-ideas leaves the room with none. The mechanism is that points compete for the same attention, and a slide of paragraph text is read instead of heard. Fixing one core message with three to five evidenced points, signposted and rehearsed to time, is what survives."},"keys":["6x6 rule","Core message","Opening moves","Transition","AIDA in talks","Speaking pace"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-73","q":"“The success with which you handle oral reporting may determine whether you are","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"tw-79","q":"You have to give a presentation on one of the occasions at college. Write a short speech","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10},{"id":"tw-10","q":"What are the characteristics of an effective presentation?","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":5},{"id":"tw-57","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":10,"name":"Recommendation Reports","rank":11,"q":2,"m":20,"share":3,"cum":97,"hook":"The ward must choose one of three sites for a water tank, and the council meets on Friday.","idea":"A recommendation report hands a decision to someone who was not present, so it must carry the reasoning in the order the reader will test it. Criteria come before options, because criteria chosen after seeing the options are only justification. Findings compare options against criteria with evidence, conclusions say what the comparison means, and recommendations say what to do, in what order, accepting which trade-off. Criteria written after the options are seen can only justify a preference already held, so they stop being evidence and become advocacy; fixing cost, time, risk and support first keeps the decision-maker arguing about the standard once instead of re-litigating each site.","one":"Criteria before options, evidence before opinion, and one recommendation the reader can act on without you.","gate":{"q":"Why must the criteria be fixed before the options are examined?","a":"Criteria written after the options are seen can only justify a preference already held, so they stop being evidence and become advocacy. Fixing cost, time, risk and support first lets every option be scored against the same standard, and lets the decision-maker argue about the standard once rather than re-litigating each site. The consequence is a recommendation the absent reader can test."},"keys":["Recommendation report","Feasibility report","Criteria","Options x criteria matrix","Executive summary","Memo of transmittal"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-13","q":"What is a recommendation report and how does it differ from a proposal? Your IT consulting firm has been asked by a client to evaluate three different cloud storage solutions (Google Drive, Dropbox, and Microsoft OneDrive) and recommend the best option for their 50-employee business. Write a recommendation report that includes problem statement, evaluation criteria, detailed analysis of each option, and your recommendation with proper justification.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10},{"id":"tw-80","q":"Write a recommendation report to Kathmandu Metropolitian City in which you suggest","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":11,"name":"Proposals","rank":12,"q":2,"m":20,"share":3,"cum":100,"hook":"A training institute promises a bank three days of Excel training, and names the price before being asked.","idea":"A proposal is a promise with a price, written so the reader can say yes without a meeting. A recommendation report only tells the reader what to decide; accepting it commissions nobody, while accepting a proposal commissions the writer. That means the problem in the reader's own words, the work described concretely enough to be checked, a schedule, named people, an itemized budget with its assumptions, and acceptance criteria that define finished. Persuasion comes from evidence and a realistic price, not from adjectives.","one":"A proposal is a promise with a price: this work, by these people, by this date, for this sum.","gate":{"q":"How does a proposal differ from a recommendation report on the same problem?","a":"A recommendation report analyses options and tells the reader what to decide, while a proposal promises that the writer will do specific work for a stated price. So the proposal carries deliverables, method, milestones, named staff, an itemised budget with assumptions and acceptance criteria. The difference matters because a recommendation can be accepted without anyone being commissioned, and a proposal cannot."},"keys":["Proposal","Solicited proposal","Unsolicited proposal","Internal proposal","Statement of work","Gantt chart"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-43","q":"Suppose you are working as an IT expert in the National Bank of Nepal, and working staff need five laptops for daily operation. Your manager asks you to present a proposal with time, cost, and objectives. Write a short proposal comparing the laptops available in the market of at least three renowned companies. Your proposal must include problems, solutions, and findings.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10},{"id":"tw-81","q":"Write a proposal convincing your parents or guardians to allow you to go with friends on the vacation of your dreams","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10}],"reps":[]}]},"ecom":{"name":"E-Commerce","code":"CSC381","exam":"2026-12-27","totalQ":69,"totalM":435,"units":[{"n":2,"name":"E-commerce Business Model","rank":1,"q":12,"m":85,"share":20,"cum":20,"hook":"The shop sells one egg at a time until a restaurant asks for two hundred, invoiced at month end.","idea":"A business model is one sentence about who pays whom, for what, and why they keep paying. An e-tailer buys stock, holds it and keeps the margin between cost and price, so unsold inventory is its own loss; a market creator never owns the goods and charges sellers a fee or commission on completed trades, so its revenue follows volume while stock risk stays with the seller. That is why marketplaces grow on thin capital, and why the eight elements — value proposition, revenue model, market, rivals, advantage, strategy, organisation and team — decide whether the sentence survives the second year.","one":"A business model is one sentence about who pays whom and why they keep paying; the eight elements make it true.","gate":{"q":"Why does a market creator carry less inventory risk than an e-tailer while earning only a fee?","a":"An e-tailer buys stock, holds it and keeps the margin between cost and price, so unsold inventory is its own loss. A market creator never owns the goods and charges sellers a fee or commission on completed trades, so its revenue follows volume while stock risk stays with the seller. That is why marketplaces grow on thin capital."},"keys":["Business model","Value proposition","Revenue model","Competitive advantage","E-tailer","Market creator"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-15","q":"What is B 2 B e-commerce? Discuss various B 2 B business models.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-25","q":"What is competitive advantage in a business model? How can firms achieve competitive advantage using dimensions of asymmetry, complementary resources, perfect market and leverage?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-37","q":"What is e-commerce business model? Describe the key elements of e-commerce business model.","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-51","q":"What is EDI? How EDI is used in ecommerce. How EDI has influenced information flow in international trade?","y":"E-commerce Question 2076","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Electronic Payment System","rank":2,"q":11,"m":85,"share":20,"cum":39,"hook":"At a Daraz checkout the card number crosses six hands, and every one of them could copy it.","idea":"An e-payment moves value through a chain: cardholder, merchant, gateway, acquiring bank, association, issuing bank. SET splits the purchase into order information for the shop and payment information for the bank, and the cardholder signs the two digests together, so nobody on the chain sees the whole purchase and nobody can repudiate their half. The bank must read the card and the merchant must read the order, yet neither may read both, so altering either half breaks verification and no party can deny the half it sent.","one":"SET is a double envelope: the shop reads the order, the bank reads the card, one seal proves they belong together.","gate":{"q":"Why does SET need a dual signature rather than one signature over the whole purchase?","a":"The merchant must read the order and the bank must read the card, yet neither may read both. So the cardholder hashes each half, signs the two digests together, and sends that one signature attached to each half. Altering either half breaks verification, and no party can deny the half it sent."},"keys":["Payment gateway","Issuer vs acquirer","Authorisation","Capture","Settlement","Dual signature"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-13","q":"What is a dual signature? Describe the dual signature operations? How is dual signature used during purchase request operation of SET protocol?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-2","q":"How does e-cheque work? Consider you are planning for an e-commerce startup facilitating an auctioning service. Explain the possible types of auctioning mechanisms with their use cases that you can facilitate in your platform.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-26","q":"What is a digital wallet? How does it work? Describe the payment authorization and payment capture operations of SET.","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-39","q":"What is e-payment? Discuss how credit card transactions, electronic checks and stored value","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":10}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Security in E-Commerce","rank":3,"q":11,"m":70,"share":16,"cum":55,"hook":"Six things can go wrong with one deposit, and a camera only stops one of them.","idea":"Security is a chain of custody judged on six properties: confidentiality, integrity, availability, authenticity, non-repudiation and privacy. Each property names a specific harm and each harm names a mechanism, so the chain holds only where every link has its own lock. A hash is a one-way digest, so altering a single digit of the amount produces a completely different value and tampering becomes visible; but anyone can compute that same digest, so it says nothing about who wrote the message. Only encrypting the digest with the sender's private key, verified through a certificate, adds authorship and non-repudiation.","one":"Security is a chain of custody with six properties, and it breaks at the one link you did not lock.","gate":{"q":"Why does a hash prove integrity but not authenticity?","a":"A hash is a one-way digest, so altering a single digit of the amount produces a completely different value and tampering becomes visible. But anyone can compute that same digest, so it says nothing about who wrote the message. Only encrypting the digest with the sender's private key, verified through a certificate, adds authorship and non-repudiation."},"keys":["Confidentiality","Non-repudiation","Digital signature","Digital certificate","PKI","Pharming"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-3","q":"How are digital signatures used in e-commerce security? How often are social engineering attacks vulnerable to e-commerce? Being an e-commerce security expert, how can you combat social engineering attacks?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-38","q":"What are the security threats in e-commerce? How client server and data transactions securities are maintained in e-commerce?","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-59","q":"Why it is essential to enforce security in e-commerce? Discuss the different dimensions of e-commerce security from consumer and merchant perspectives.","y":"E-commerce Question 2079","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-20","q":"How cryptography and hash functions are used in e-commerce to ensure security.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Digital Marketing","rank":4,"q":11,"m":65,"share":15,"cum":70,"hook":"The hoarding above the paan shop is rented by the month and nobody knows who it brings in.","idea":"Digital marketing rents attention by the unit that can be counted and lets an auction set the price for each unit. Targeting decides whose attention is worth bidding for, the exchange sells it impression by impression, and acquisition cost measured against lifetime value decides whether the whole exercise made money. Click-through rate says only whether the creative earned attention, never whether the visit returned anything, so a channel whose acquisition cost exceeds lifetime value loses money on every sale however healthy its click rate looks.","one":"Digital marketing rents attention by the countable unit, at a price an auction sets in milliseconds.","gate":{"q":"Why must a campaign be judged on acquisition cost against lifetime value rather than click-through rate?","a":"Click-through rate says only whether the creative earned attention, never whether the visit returned anything. Acquisition cost divides total spend by customers won, while lifetime value is what those customers return over their whole relationship. A channel whose cost exceeds that value loses money on every sale, however healthy its click rate looks."},"keys":["CTR","CPM","eCPM","RTB","DSP vs SSP","Quality score"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-14","q":"What is digital marketing? Why is it essential in e-commerce? How do marketing metrics like click-through rate, bounce-back rate and conversion rate influence E-mail marketing?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-27","q":"What is display ad marketing? How attrition rate, view-to-cart ratio, acquisition rate and retention rate influence display ad marketing?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-10","q":"How are reach, loyalty, and hits used in display ad marketing metrics?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-22","q":"How social marketing, mobile marketing and local marketing are used in e-commerce to promote business.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5}],"reps":[]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","rank":5,"q":10,"m":60,"share":14,"cum":84,"hook":"A kirana shop in Asan needs no website; take away its four walls and it needs a law, a courier and a bank.","idea":"E-commerce is the same shop with its walls replaced by five layers that must all work: people, public policy, marketing, support services and partnerships. A website only publishes an offer, while completing a sale still needs people to staff it, law to enforce the record, marketing to be found, support services to move money and goods, and partners to supply the stock. Remove any layer and the order dies at that point, which is why cash on delivery dominates wherever the payment layer is thin.","one":"E-commerce removes the shop's walls and replaces them with five layers that must all hold at once.","gate":{"q":"Why does e-commerce need a five-layer framework rather than simply a website and a delivery van?","a":"A website only publishes an offer, while completing a sale still needs people to staff it, law to enforce the record, marketing to be found, support services to move money and goods, and partners to supply the stock. Remove any layer and the order dies at that point, which is why cash on delivery dominates wherever the payment layer is thin."},"keys":["E-commerce","E-business","Ubiquity","Information density","Richness","Pure e-commerce"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-1","q":"How are people, public policy, and support services shaping the e-commerce framework? How can organizations adapt e-distribution and e-procurement to make their B2B transactions better?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-49","q":"What is media convergence in ecommerce? How it influence ecommerce? Discuss technological and market factors that drive media convergence.","y":"E-commerce Question 2076","marks":10},{"id":"ecom-24","q":"Briefly explain the electronic transaction act of Nepal.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-36","q":"How provisions relating to digital certificates are defined in the electronic transaction act of Nepal?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r2","q":"How are provisions relating to the Information Technology Tribunal defined in the Electronic Transaction Act of Nepal?","count":3,"marks":5}]},{"n":7,"name":"Optimizing E-commerce Systems","rank":6,"q":8,"m":40,"share":9,"cum":93,"hook":"A librarian who has read everything decides which shelf you meet first, and notices what you take next.","idea":"Optimisation raises the odds that a given visitor buys, once before arrival by being crawled, indexed and ranked, and once after arrival by being shown the next plausible item. Collaborative filtering needs a user-item matrix, so a visitor with no history has no neighbours to borrow from, and a new item nobody bought has none either; content-based filtering compares item features with what that visitor already viewed, so it recommends before any community behaviour exists. Hybrid systems fall back on popularity or attributes until history accumulates, and both halves are measured, never assumed.","one":"SEO convinces the librarian to shelve you near the door; recommendation notices what other borrowers took with your book.","gate":{"q":"Why does collaborative filtering fail a new visitor while content-based filtering still works?","a":"Collaborative filtering needs a user-item matrix, so a visitor with no history has no neighbours to borrow from, and a new item nobody bought has none either. Content-based filtering compares item features with what that visitor already viewed, so it recommends before any community behaviour exists. Hybrid systems fall back on popularity or attributes until history accumulates."},"keys":["Crawling","Indexing","PageRank","On-page SEO","Off-page SEO","Technical SEO"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-11","q":"What is the significance of page rank in search engine optimization? How can you increase the page rank of an e-commerce website?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-12","q":"Consider you are hired as a SEO expert. How can you use content-based and collaborative filtering approaches to recommend products?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-23","q":"Describe the working mechanism of search engines.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-35","q":"What is the significance of recommendation systems in e-commerce? How is collaborative filtering different from content based?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":5}],"reps":[{"id":"r18","q":"Consider you are hired as a SEO expert. How can you use content-based and collaborative filtering approaches to recommend products?","count":2,"marks":5},{"id":"r28","q":"Why it is essential to perform Search Engine Optimization (SEO)? How off page SEO can be done?","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":4,"name":"Building E-commerce System","rank":7,"q":6,"m":30,"share":7,"cum":100,"hook":"In a big shop the boy at the counter keeps your pile; online, every click hires a new boy.","idea":"A shopping cart is a claim slip written outside the page, so the basket survives navigation, refresh and login. Checkout freezes the slip into an order with a fixed total and a legal sequence of states. The browser's return page is only a URL the browser was told to open, so a shopper can type it, replay it, or abandon the tab before it loads; payment is proved by a server-to-server callback whose signature the store verifies and applies once through an idempotency key. A shop trusting the redirect ships goods nobody paid for.","one":"A cart is a claim slip outside the page; checkout turns it into an order only a verified callback can mark paid.","gate":{"q":"Why is the browser's return page not proof that a payment succeeded?","a":"That page is only a URL the browser was told to open, so a shopper can type it, replay it, or abandon the tab before it loads. Payment is proved by a server-to-server callback whose signature the store verifies and applies once through an idempotency key. A shop trusting the redirect ships goods nobody paid for."},"keys":["Static catalogue","Dynamic catalogue","Server-side cart","Cart merging","Order state machine","Idempotency key"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-19","q":"What are the basic elements to be considered while developing an e-commerce website?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-31","q":"What is catalog in e-commerce? How are catalogs created?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-43","q":"What is a catalog in e-commerce application? How can you create catalogs?","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":5},{"id":"ecom-52","q":"What are the different types of digital documents used in corporate library?","y":"E-commerce Question 2076","marks":5}],"reps":[]}]}}};
